 * simple, unoptimized, maybe buggy irc cloner program BUT IT ROCKS.
 *
 * because i lost previous sources, i decide to write it again from begin
 * and again based on jococloner by [joco].
 *
 * Original re-release (binary only): 2013.12.12. please don't abuse.
 *
 * == v0.3.0 (2026) - Modernization ==
 *
 * - Builds natively on modern 64-bit systems (Linux, macOS, FreeBSD).
 *   Optional --with-32bit if you still need it.
 * - OpenSSL SSL/TLS support for IRCNet 2.11+ / 2.12.0-pre servers
 *   (typical port 6697). Auto-detected at configure time.
 * - GitHub Actions CI: Ubuntu, macOS, FreeBSD.
 * - Code hygiene (vsnprintf, includes, dependencies).
 * - Full backward compatibility: plain TCP still works exactly as before.
 *
 * Build:
 *   cd src
 *   ./configure [--with-ssl] [--without-ssl] [--without-validator] [--with-32bit]
 *   make dynamic          # preferred
 *   # or: make static / make debug
 *
 * Usage:
 *   ./cloner <server> [port] [number] [delay] [ip vhost file]
 *   Default port 6667 (plain). Port 6697 or explicit SSL build uses TLS.
 *
 * SSL notes:
 *   Requires OpenSSL at build time. Certificate verification is currently
 *   disabled (common for this class of tool); can be tightened later.
 *   IRCNet servers may send "Your connection is secure (SSL/TLS)" NOTICE
 *   and WHOIS "is a Secure Connection".
 *
 * about fast mode: it's a lot faster but sometimes it can be a disatvantage.
 * when in fast mode, the cloner assumes that after it sends an op command,
 * then it doesn't wait for the irc server's answer but goes on with the opped
 * client right then. when the lags are small (check out with the lag command)
 * and you have a good connection, then fast mode is really cool! 100 clones
 * can op each other in a millisecond. the problem is when you're lagged.
 *
 * note about testing it against other similar software: when you test it on
 * small, local networks, beware: the last connected client gets processed
 * first in ircd, i.e. the process order and connect order are reverse.
 *
 * as i mentioned, it's buggy. it doesn't care about channel modes, it always
 * assumes the clients are not banned, channel can be joined, etc.
 *
 * See CHANGELOG for full history. Work continues on the develop branch.
